@@@@ mark-error-refresh-remainder-sign
situation: mark-error
topic: Integer division and remainder with negative operands in C
options: {"refresh":true}
voice: wordy
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"partial","accept":["wrong"],"names":["always between 0 and 4|-17 % 5 = 3","sign of the dividend"],"must":["-17 % 5 = -2","Problem 3"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"askMustNot":["-5\\b","-3\\b"]}
@@ tutor
**Problem 2**

Evaluate each of these in C: `-17 / 5`, `-17 % 5`, `17 / -5` and `17 % -5`. Then give the value of `((-17 % 5) + 5) % 5`.

<solution>-17 / 5 = -3 (truncation towards zero). -17 % 5 = -17 - (-3)(5) = -2. 17 / -5 = -3. 17 % -5 = 17 - (-3)(-5) = 2. Last: (-2 + 5) % 5 = 3.</solution>
<check lang="python">
import math
cdiv = lambda a, b: math.trunc(a / b)
cmod = lambda a, b: a - cdiv(a, b) * b
assert [cdiv(-17, 5), cmod(-17, 5), cdiv(17, -5), cmod(17, -5)] == [-3, -2, -3, 2]
assert cmod(cmod(-17, 5) + 5, 5) == 3
</check>

<ask type="problem">The five values, with your working.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
OK so division in C truncates toward zero, which means -17 / 5 = -3. For the remainder, a remainder is always between 0 and 4 when you divide by 5, so -17 % 5 = 3. Next 17 / -5 = -3, again by truncation. Then 17 % -5 I worked out from the identity: 17 - (-3)(-5) = 17 - 15 = 2. And the last one is (3 + 5) % 5 = 8 % 5 = 3.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="partial" score="0.6">Both quotients are right, "-17 / 5 = -3" and "17 / -5 = -3". The first wrong step is "a remainder is always between 0 and 4 when you divide by 5, so -17 % 5 = 3": in C the remainder takes the sign of the dividend, so -17 % 5 = -2.</grade>

The identity you used on the next line gives it directly: -17 - (-3)(5) = -2. Your "17 - (-3)(-5) = 17 - 15 = 2" is right. The last line comes out as 3 either way, because (-2 + 5) % 5 = 3: that expression exists to turn C's remainder into the never-negative one you assumed `%` gives, so it is not a second error.

<card>In C, what is -17 % 5, and what decides its sign? :: -2. The quotient truncates towards zero (-3), and a % b = a - (a / b) * b, so the remainder has the sign of the dividend.</card>

<note>C (C99 on): `a / b` truncates towards zero and `a % b == a - (a / b) * b`, so a non-zero remainder has the sign of `a`. For a result in 0 to n - 1, use `((a % n) + n) % n`.</note>

**Problem 3**

Evaluate `-23 / 4`, `-23 % 4` and `23 % -4`, then `((-23 % 4) + 4) % 4`.

<solution>-23 / 4 = -5. -23 % 4 = -23 - (-5)(4) = -3. 23 / -4 = -5, so 23 % -4 = 23 - (-5)(-4) = 3. Last: (-3 + 4) % 4 = 1.</solution>
<check lang="python">
import math
cdiv = lambda a, b: math.trunc(a / b)
cmod = lambda a, b: a - cdiv(a, b) * b
assert [cdiv(-23, 4), cmod(-23, 4), cmod(23, -4), cmod(cmod(-23, 4) + 4, 4)] == [-5, -3, 3, 1]
</check>

<ask type="problem">The four values. Use the identity for each remainder and show it.</ask>
@@ verify
p = lambda body: run_c('#include <stdio.h>\nint main(void) { int a = 17, b = 5, c = 23, d = 4; (void)a; (void)b; (void)c; (void)d; ' + body + ' return 0; }\n').split()
assert p('printf("%d %d %d %d %d\\n", -a / b, -a % b, a / -b, a % -b, ((-a % b) + b) % b);') == ['-3', '-2', '-3', '2', '3']
assert p('printf("%d %d %d %d\\n", -c / d, -c % d, c % -d, ((-c % d) + d) % d);') == ['-5', '-3', '3', '1']
# the learner's lines: the slip, what follows from it, and the lines that are right
assert -17 % 5 == 3                       # the never-negative (floored) remainder they assumed
assert 17 - (-3) * (-5) == 2 and (3 + 5) % 5 == 3 and (-2 + 5) % 5 == 3
assert -17 - (-3) * 5 == -2
s = solution(reply)
for part in ('-23 / 4 = -5', '= -3', '= 3', '= 1'):
    assert part in s

@@@@ mark-error-sort-comparator-tie-break
situation: mark-error
topic: std::sort with custom comparators
voice: wordy
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"partial","accept":["wrong"],"names":["a > b","banana date kiwi plum fig"],"must":["a < b"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["code"]}
@@ tutor
**Problem 2 · harder**

Sort the words `{"kiwi", "fig", "banana", "plum", "date"}` so that longer words come first, and words of the same length are in alphabetical order. Use `std::sort` with a lambda as the comparator, and print the words on one line separated by spaces.

<solution>Comparator: if (a.size() != b.size()) return a.size() > b.size(); return a < b; Output: banana date kiwi plum fig</solution>

<ask type="code" lang="cpp">Write the whole program and say what it prints.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<std::string> w = {"kiwi", "fig", "banana", "plum", "date"};
    std::sort(w.begin(), w.end(), [](const std::string &a, const std::string &b) {
        if (a.size() != b.size()) return a.size() > b.size();
        return a > b;
    });
    for (const auto &s : w) std::cout << s << " ";
    std::cout << "\n";
    return 0;
}
```
It prints banana plum kiwi date fig. The way I thought about it: the lambda first looks at the two lengths, and if they are different it uses > so the longer word goes first. If the lengths are the same it falls through to the plain string comparison, which handles the ties.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="partial" score="0.6">The length test is right: `return a.size() > b.size();` puts longer words first, and it is only reached when the sizes differ. The wrong line is `return a > b;`: for equal lengths that orders the larger string first, which is reverse alphabetical, so the three four-letter words come out as plum kiwi date. With `return a < b;` the program prints banana date kiwi plum fig.</grade>

The comparator answers one question: must `a` come before `b`. Each line has to be read that way, and for the tie that means `a < b`.

<card>In a std::sort comparator, what should the tie-break return for ascending alphabetical order? :: `a < b`: the comparator returns true exactly when a must come before b.</card>

<note>A `std::sort` comparator `cmp(a, b)` returns true exactly when `a` must come before `b`. For two keys: compare the first key when it differs, otherwise return the comparison of the second key in the direction you want.</note>

**Problem 3 · same level**

<solution>Comparator: if (std::abs(a) != std::abs(b)) return std::abs(a) < std::abs(b); return a < b; Output: -1 1 -2 2 -3 3</solution>

<ask type="code" lang="cpp">Sort `{3, -1, 2, -3, 1, -2}` by absolute value, smallest first, with the negative number first when two absolute values are equal. Use `std::sort` with a lambda and print the result on one line.</ask>
@@ verify
out = run_cpp(block(learner, 'cpp')).split()
assert out == ['banana', 'plum', 'kiwi', 'date', 'fig'], out          # what the learner's program really prints
fixed = block(learner, 'cpp').replace('return a > b;', 'return a < b;')
assert fixed != block(learner, 'cpp')
assert run_cpp(fixed).split() == ['banana', 'date', 'kiwi', 'plum', 'fig']
assert sorted(['kiwi', 'fig', 'banana', 'plum', 'date'], key=lambda s: (-len(s), s)) == ['banana', 'date', 'kiwi', 'plum', 'fig']
ref = r'''
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
int main() {
    std::vector<int> v = {3, -1, 2, -3, 1, -2};
    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int a, int b) {
        if (std::abs(a) != std::abs(b)) return std::abs(a) < std::abs(b);
        return a < b;
    });
    for (int x : v) std::cout << x << " ";
    std::cout << "\n";
    return 0;
}
'''
assert run_cpp(ref).split() == ['-1', '1', '-2', '2', '-3', '3']
assert 'Output: -1 1 -2 2 -3 3' in solution(reply)

@@@@ mark-error-short-circuit-or-evaluates-right
situation: mark-error
topic: Short-circuit evaluation in C
options: {"difficulty":"hard"}
voice: plain
level: hard
expect: {"verdict":"partial","accept":["wrong"],"names":["right side has to be evaluated|b becomes 6","b = 5|b stays 5|b is still 5"],"must":["b = 5"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"askMustNot":["x = 1","y = 2"]}
@@ tutor
**Problem 2 · hard**

```c
int a = 0, b = 5;
int r = a++ && b++;
int s = a++ || b++;
```

Give the final values of `a`, `b`, `r` and `s`.

<solution>Line 2: a++ yields 0 (a becomes 1); the left operand of && is false, so b++ is not evaluated and r = 0. Line 3: a++ yields 1 (a becomes 2); the left operand of || is true, so b++ is not evaluated and s = 1. Final: a = 2, b = 5, r = 0, s = 1.</solution>
<check lang="python">
a, b = 0, 5
left = a; a += 1
if left: right = b; b += 1; r = int(bool(right))
else: r = 0
left = a; a += 1
if left: s = 1
else: right = b; b += 1; s = int(bool(right))
assert (a, b, r, s) == (2, 5, 0, 1)
</check>

<ask type="problem">The four final values, line by line.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
Line 2: a++ gives 0 and then a is 1. 0 is false so && stops there, b++ never runs, r = 0.
Line 3: a++ gives 1 and then a is 2. The left side is true, so now the right side has to be evaluated as well: b++ gives 5 and b becomes 6. 1 || 5 is 1, so s = 1.
Final: a = 2, b = 6, r = 0, s = 1.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="partial" score="0.6">Line 2 is right, including "0 is false so && stops there, b++ never runs". The first wrong step is on line 3: "The left side is true, so now the right side has to be evaluated as well". That is the rule for `&&`. For `||` a true left operand already decides the result, so `b++` is skipped and b = 5.</grade>

Your `a = 2`, `r = 0` and `s = 1` stand; only `b` changes. The corrected result is a = 2, b = 5, r = 0, s = 1.

<card>When does `x || y` evaluate y in C? :: Only when x is false (zero). A true left operand decides the result as 1 and y is not evaluated, side effects included.</card>

<note>`x && y` evaluates `y` only when `x` is true; `x || y` evaluates `y` only when `x` is false. A skipped operand's side effects (`b++`, a function call) do not happen.</note>

**Problem 3 · same level**

```c
int x = 3, y = 0;
int p = y++ || x--;
int q = y++ && x--;
```

<solution>Line 2: y++ yields 0 (y becomes 1); false, so || evaluates x--, which yields 3 (x becomes 2); p = 1. Line 3: y++ yields 1 (y becomes 2); true, so && evaluates x--, which yields 2 (x becomes 1); q = 1. Final: x = 1, y = 2, p = 1, q = 1.</solution>
<check lang="python">
x, y = 3, 0
left = y; y += 1
if left: p = 1
else: right = x; x -= 1; p = int(bool(right))
left = y; y += 1
if left: right = x; x -= 1; q = int(bool(right))
else: q = 0
assert (x, y, p, q) == (1, 2, 1, 1)
</check>

<ask type="problem">The final values of x, y, p and q. For each line, say whether the right operand is evaluated.</ask>
@@ verify
run = lambda body, fmt: run_c('#include <stdio.h>\nint main(void) {\n' + body + 'printf("%d %d %d %d\\n", ' + fmt + ');\nreturn 0; }\n').split()
assert run(block(tutor[0], 'c'), 'a, b, r, s') == ['2', '5', '0', '1']
assert run(block(reply, 'c'), 'x, y, p, q') == ['1', '2', '1', '1']
assert 'x = 1, y = 2, p = 1, q = 1' in solution(reply)
# the learner's b = 6 is what follows if the right operand of || were evaluated
a, b = 2, 5; b += 1
assert b == 6

@@@@ mark-error-enum-value-after-explicit
situation: mark-error
topic: Enumerations in C
