@@ tutor
**Problem 2 · harder**

```c
enum state { IDLE, RUNNING, DONE };
```

Write `const char *state_name(enum state s)` that returns "idle", "running" or "done" for the three enumerators and "unknown" for any other value.

<solution>const char *state_name(enum state s) { switch (s) { case IDLE: return "idle"; case RUNNING: return "running"; case DONE: return "done"; } return "unknown"; }</solution>

<ask type="code" lang="c">Write the function.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
```c
const char *state_name(enum state s) {
    static const char *const names[] = { "idle", "running", "done" };
    if ((unsigned)s >= sizeof names / sizeof names[0]) return "unknown";
    return names[s];
}
```
I didn't use a switch here. The enumerators are 0, 1 and 2 because none of them has an explicit value, so I can use them directly as positions in an array of strings. The cast to unsigned is there so that a negative value turns into a huge number and gets caught by the same range check as a too-large one.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">This is correct. "The enumerators are 0, 1 and 2" is what makes `names[s]` a valid lookup, and `(unsigned)s >= sizeof names / sizeof names[0]` rejects both a value above 2 and a negative one, as you say, before the array is indexed.</grade>

The table depends on the strings staying in the same order as the enumerators; a `switch` over the enum does not, and compilers can warn when a `switch` misses an enumerator. Either is a sound choice here.

**Problem 3 · harder**

```c
enum perm { READ = 1, WRITE = 2, EXEC = 4 };

int p = READ | EXEC;
```

<solution>READ | EXEC = 001 | 100 in binary = 101 = 5. p & WRITE = 101 & 010 = 0, so the WRITE bit is not set. p | WRITE = 111 = 7.</solution>
<check lang="python">
READ, WRITE, EXEC = 1, 2, 4
p = READ | EXEC
assert (p, p & WRITE, p | WRITE) == (5, 0, 7)
</check>

<ask type="problem">Give the values of `p`, `p & WRITE` and `p | WRITE`, and say why the enumerators were given the values 1, 2 and 4 rather than 0, 1 and 2.</ask>
@@ verify
src = '#include <stdio.h>\n' + block(tutor[0], 'c') + block(learner, 'c') + r'''
int main(void) {
    printf("%s %s %s %s %s\n", state_name(IDLE), state_name(RUNNING), state_name(DONE), state_name((enum state)3), state_name((enum state)-1));
    return 0;
}
'''
assert run_c(src).split() == ['idle', 'running', 'done', 'unknown', 'unknown']
# the reference switch gives the same answers
ref = '#include <stdio.h>\n' + block(tutor[0], 'c') + solution(tutor[0]) + r'''
int main(void) {
    printf("%s %s %s %s\n", state_name(IDLE), state_name(RUNNING), state_name(DONE), state_name((enum state)3));
    return 0;
}
'''
assert run_c(ref).split() == ['idle', 'running', 'done', 'unknown']
out = run_c('#include <stdio.h>\n' + block(reply, 'c').replace('int p = READ | EXEC;', '') + 'int main(void) { int p = READ | EXEC; printf("%d %d %d\\n", p, p & WRITE, p | WRITE); return 0; }\n')
assert out.split() == ['5', '0', '7']
# clang does warn when a switch over the enum misses an enumerator
out, err, cerr, code = run_c_full(block(tutor[0], 'c') + 'int f(enum state s) { switch (s) { case IDLE: return 1; case RUNNING: return 2; } return 0; }\nint main(void) { return f(DONE); }\n', 'c')
assert 'not handled in switch' in cerr, cerr

@@@@ tangent-why-vector-doubles
situation: tangent
topic: std::vector growth and capacity
voice: typos
level: medium
expect: {"must":["amortised","49,500","1,023"],"askTypes":["short"],"askMustNot":["\\b5\\b","five"]}
@@ tutor
`size()` is the number of elements a vector holds; `capacity()` is how many fit in the buffer it has now. When a `push_back` would take the size past the capacity, the vector allocates a larger buffer, moves the elements across and frees the old one.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v;
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        v.push_back(i);
        std::cout << v.size() << "/" << v.capacity() << " ";
    }
    std::cout << "\n";
    return 0;
}
```

```
1/1 2/2 3/4 4/4 5/8
```

This standard library (libc++) doubles the capacity each time. The growth factor is the implementation's choice, not fixed by the standard.

<note>`size()`: elements stored. `capacity()`: elements the current buffer can hold. A `push_back` at full capacity allocates a larger buffer and moves every element; libc++ and libstdc++ double the capacity.</note>

**Problem 1 · warm-up**

An empty vector grows by doubling, as above. How many times does it allocate a new buffer during 9 `push_back` calls?

<solution>The capacity goes 1, 2, 4, 8, 16: the buffers are allocated at the 1st, 2nd, 3rd, 5th and 9th push_back. That is 5 allocations.</solution>
<check lang="python">
cap, allocs = 0, 0
for size in range(1, 10):
    if size > cap:
        cap = max(1, cap * 2); allocs += 1
assert (cap, allocs) == (16, 5)
</check>

<ask type="problem">The number of allocations, with the capacity after each one.</ask>
@@ learner question
side question before i anwser, why double it? wouldnt adding like 10 slots each time waste way less memory
@@ reply
It would waste less memory and cost far more time. Growing by a fixed 10 slots means a new buffer every 10 `push_back` calls, and each new buffer has every element stored so far moved into it. Over 1,000 calls that is 10 + 20 + ... + 990 = 49,500 element moves, and the total grows with the square of the number of elements. With doubling the moves are 1 + 2 + 4 + ... + 512 = 1,023, always fewer than twice the number of elements. That is what makes `push_back` amortised constant time, which the standard requires. The price is spare capacity, up to about half the buffer, and some libraries grow by a factor of 1.5 to waste less.

<ask type="short">Back to the problem: under doubling, write down the capacity after each of the 9 `push_back` calls, then count how many times it changed.</ask>
@@ verify
assert run_cpp(block(tutor[0], 'cpp')).strip() == '1/1 2/2 3/4 4/4 5/8'
def moves(n, grow):
    cap, moved = 0, 0
    for size in range(1, n + 1):
        if size > cap:
            moved += size - 1          # everything stored so far goes to the new buffer
            cap = grow(cap)
    return moved
fixed = moves(1000, lambda c: c + 10)
doubling = moves(1000, lambda c: max(1, c * 2))
assert fixed == sum(range(10, 1000, 10)) == 49500
assert doubling == sum(2**k for k in range(10)) == 1023
for n in (10, 1000, 12345, 100000):
    assert moves(n, lambda c: max(1, c * 2)) < 2 * n
# quadratic growth of the fixed-step total: ten times the elements, about a hundred times the moves
assert 95 < moves(10000, lambda c: c + 10) / fixed < 105
# the capacities over 9 push_backs, as this library really grows
out = run_cpp(block(tutor[0], 'cpp').replace('i < 5', 'i < 9')).split()
caps = [int(x.split('/')[1]) for x in out]
assert caps == [1, 2, 4, 4, 8, 8, 8, 8, 16] and len(set(caps)) == 5

@@@@ tangent-pragma-once
situation: tangent
topic: Header guards in C
voice: typos
level: easy
expect: {"must":["not part of the C or C\\+\\+ standard","GCC, Clang and MSVC"],"askTypes":["short","problem","predict"],"askMustNot":["redefinition","twice|two definitions"]}
@@ tutor
An include guard wraps the whole of a header in `#ifndef POINT_H`, `#define POINT_H` and a closing `#endif`. The first time the header is pasted into a translation unit the macro is not yet defined, so the contents are kept and the macro becomes defined. Any later `#include` of the same header in that translation unit finds the macro defined and skips everything up to the `#endif`.

<note>Include guard: `#ifndef NAME_H` / `#define NAME_H` / contents / `#endif`. The contents are seen once per translation unit, however many times the header is included.</note>

**Problem 1 · warm-up**

`point.h` defines `struct point { int x, y; };` and has no guard. `shapes.h` and `canvas.h` each begin with `#include "point.h"`. `main.c` includes `shapes.h` and then `canvas.h`.

<solution>After preprocessing, main.c contains the definition of struct point twice: once through shapes.h and once through canvas.h. The compiler reports a redefinition of struct point at the second one, reached through canvas.h.</solution>

<ask type="problem">What does the compiler report for `main.c`, and at which include does it happen?</ask>
@@ learner question
quick one first. ive seen #pragma once at the top of headers instaed of the ifndef lines, is that the same thing and is it proper C?
@@ reply
It has the same effect: `#pragma once` tells the compiler to include that file at most once in a translation unit. It is not part of the C or C++ standard, but GCC, Clang and MSVC all support it, so in practice it works almost everywhere. The two fail in different ways. `#pragma once` depends on the compiler recognising that two paths lead to the same file, which can go wrong when a header is reachable as two copies or through links. A guard depends only on its macro name, which goes wrong if two headers choose the same name.

<ask type="short">Back to `main.c`, where `point.h` has neither. Once the preprocessor has pasted in `shapes.h` and then `canvas.h`, how many definitions of `struct point` does the compiler see in the translation unit, and what does it say about that?</ask>
@@ verify
import subprocess, tempfile, os
def build(point_h):
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as d:
        w = lambda n, s: open(os.path.join(d, n), 'w').write(s)
        w('point.h', point_h)
        w('shapes.h', '#include "point.h"\nstruct circle { struct point centre; int r; };\n')
        w('canvas.h', '#include "point.h"\nstruct canvas { struct point origin; };\n')
        w('main.c', '#include "shapes.h"\n#include "canvas.h"\nint main(void) { struct circle c = {{0, 0}, 1}; struct canvas k = {{0, 0}}; return c.r - 1 + k.origin.x; }\n')
        p = subprocess.run(['/usr/bin/clang', '-std=c17', '-Wall', '-Wextra', 'main.c', '-o', 'p'], cwd=d, capture_output=True, text=True)
        return p.returncode, p.stderr
body = 'struct point { int x, y; };\n'
code, err = build(body)
assert code != 0 and "redefinition of 'point'" in err and 'canvas.h' in err, err
assert build('#ifndef POINT_H\n#define POINT_H\n' + body + '#endif\n') == (0, '')
assert build('#pragma once\n' + body) == (0, '')

@@@@ tangent-bit-field-layout-on-the-wire
situation: tangent
topic: Bit-fields in C
voice: confused
level: hard
expect: {"must":["implementation-defined","shifts and masks"],"askTypes":["short","problem"],"askMustNot":["\\b5\\b","\\b6\\b"]}
@@ tutor
```c
struct flags {
    unsigned ready : 1;
    unsigned mode  : 3;
    unsigned count : 4;
};
```

A bit-field is an integer member that occupies a stated number of bits. `mode : 3` can hold 0 to 7 and `count : 4` can hold 0 to 15. Storing a value that does not fit in an unsigned bit-field keeps only its low bits: the value is reduced modulo $2^{w}$ for a field of width $w$.

<note>An unsigned bit-field of width $w$ holds 0 to $2^w - 1$; a larger value stored in it is reduced modulo $2^w$.</note>

**Problem 1 · warm-up**

With `struct flags f;`, the program runs `f.mode = 13;` and `f.count = 22;`.

<solution>mode is 3 bits wide: 13 mod 8 = 5. count is 4 bits wide: 22 mod 16 = 6.</solution>
<check lang="python">
assert 13 % 2**3 == 5 and 22 % 2**4 == 6
</check>

<ask type="problem">What do `f.mode` and `f.count` hold afterwards? Show the reduction for each.</ask>
@@ learner question
wait, so is ready the lowest bit of the first byte or the highest one? I assumed I could lay a struct like this over a packet header straight off the wire and read the fields, but now I'm not sure which bit ends up where
@@ reply
Not reliably. The C standard leaves the order in which bit-fields are placed within a storage unit implementation-defined, along with whether a field may straddle two units. On the common little-endian targets (x86-64 and AArch64 with GCC or Clang) the first field does land in the least significant bit, but big-endian ABIs typically start from the most significant end, so the same struct describes a different byte there. For a wire format, read the bytes into a fixed-width unsigned integer and extract each field with shifts and masks, which give the same result under every compiler.

<ask type="short">Back to the widths: a 3-bit field can hold eight different values and a 4-bit field sixteen. What is left of 13 in `mode`, and of 22 in `count`, once each has been stored?</ask>
@@ verify
src = '#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n' + block(tutor[0], 'c') + r'''
int main(void) {
    struct flags f;
    memset(&f, 0, sizeof f);
    f.mode = 13;
    f.count = 22;
    printf("%u %u\n", f.mode, f.count);
    memset(&f, 0, sizeof f);
    f.ready = 1;
    unsigned char first;
    memcpy(&first, &f, 1);
    printf("%u\n", first);
    return 0;
}
'''
out, err, cerr, code = run_c_full(src, 'c')
assert code == 0 and out.split() == ['5', '6', '1'], (out, cerr)     # on this little-endian target, ready is bit 0 of byte 0
assert 'changes value from 13 to 5' in cerr and 'changes value from 22 to 6' in cerr
assert 2**3 == 8 and 2**4 == 16
# shifts and masks decode the same byte with no dependence on the compiler's layout
byte = 1 | (5 << 1) | (6 << 4)
assert (byte & 1, (byte >> 1) & 0b111, (byte >> 4) & 0b1111) == (1, 5, 6)
